esh\n \nSheopur\n \nSheopurkalan\n \n75\n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \nTikamgarh\n \nTikamgarh\n \n76\n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \nUjjain\n \nBadnagar\n \n77\n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \nUjjain\n \nMahidpur\n \n78\n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \nUjjain\n \nUjjain\n \n79\n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \nVidisha\n \nGanj Basoda\n \n80\n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \nVidisha\n \nVidisha\n \n \n*******\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S5_U_30": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 30 \n  ANSWERED ON 02.02.2021 \n \nIMPLEMENTATION OF MGNREGS \n \n30.  DR. KALANIDHI VEERASWAMY: \nSHRI KAPIL MORESHWAR PATIL: \nSHRI KRUPAL BALAJI TUMANE: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT  be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the salient features of the Mahatma Gandhi Nat ional Rural Employment Guarantee \nScheme(MGNREGS) alongwith the funds allocated, sanc tioned and utilised during the \nlast three years and the current year in the countr y, State-wise including Maharastra, \nRajasthan, Gujarat and Tamilnadu; \n(b)  the number of persons seeking employment and w orkers/persons registered/employment \ngenerated under MGNREGS during the said period, alo ng with the step taken for the \nworkers who got affected due to COVID-19 crisis/loc kdown in the country, State-wise, \nincluding Tamilnadu; \n(c)  whether the Government is planning to increase   the number of persons covered under \nthe scheme and the funds/budget allocation due to i ncrease in work demand and \nproviding more works for boosting the rural economy  in the current year and if so, the \ndetails thereof; \n(d)  whether any provision has been made for SC/ST community under the scheme, if so, the \ndetails thereof along with the achievement made in this regard; and \n(e)  whether cases of violation of the criteria fix ed for the backward classes have come to the \nnotice of the Government, if so, the details thereof and the action taken in this regard?  \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a)      \nThe salient features of the Mahatma Gandhi National  Rural Employment Guarantee \nScheme (Mahatma Gandhi NREGS) are detailed at Annexure-I.  \n  \nMahatma Gandhi NREGS is a demand driven wage employ ment scheme. Hence no State/UT-\nwise allocation of funds is made.  Fund release to the States/UTs including Maharastra, \nRajasthan, Gujarat and Tamilnadu is a continuous pr ocess. Central Government is committed to \nmaking funds available keeping in view the demand f or work. States/UTs-wise details of central \nfunds released and total expenditure, including for  the States of Maharastra, Rajasthan, Gujarat \nand Tamilnadu, during the last three years and the current FY 2020-21 (as on 28.01.2021) under \nMahatma Gandhi NREGS is given at Annexure-II . \n (b)      As per information uploaded by the States/ UTs including Tamil Nadu in the State/UT-\nwise number of persons demanded Employment, number of persons registered and number of  \npersons provided employment during the last three y ears and the current FY 2020-21 (as on \n28.01.2021) under Mahatma Gandhi NREGS is given at Annexure-III .\n \n  \n\nMahatma Gandhi NREGA proved to be a major support t o the returning migrant workers \nduring COVID-19 Pandemic mitigating the adverse of effects thereof. During the financial year, \nmore than 1.57 crore new job cards have been issued . A rural household, adult members of \nwhich agree to unskilled manual work, is entitled f or hundred days of wage employment. During \nFY 2020-21 (as on 31.12.2020), more than 292.62 crore person-days have been generated, which \nis approximately 47% more than the employment gener ated during the same period in FY 2019-\n20. The agreed-to labour budget of 366 crore (as on  31.12.2020) for the current financial year is \nthe highest ever allocation under the programme.  \n  \nThe Central Government, with active support of the State Governments, has so far been \nable to meet the demands of works with adequate fun ding. During current financial year, as on \n28.01.2021, the Ministry has released Rs. 89231.53  crore to the States & UTs for implementation \nof Scheme and a total of 314.75 crore Persondays ha ve been generated so far against revised \napproved Labour Budget 336.04  crore Persondays.  \n  \n(c)      The Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employme nt Guarantee Scheme (Mahatma Gandhi \nNREGS), is a demand driven wage employment programm e. A rural household, adult members \nof which agree to unskilled manual work, is entitle d for hundred days of wage employment. \nTotal number of person worked during the current FY  2020-21 (so far) is 10.28 crore which is \n30% more than that of the FY 2019-20. Hence no Stat e/UT-wise allocation of funds is made. \nFund release to the States/UTs is a continuous proc ess. Central Government is committed to \nmaking funds available keeping in view the demand f or work.  There was a budget allocation of \nRs.61,500 crore at BE for Mahatma Gandhi NREGA and Ministry of Finance has now allocated Rs. \n1,11,500 crore at RE during 2020-21.  \n  \n(d) & (e)    In addition to the prescribed benefits  under the Mahatma Gandhi NREGS, the \nMinistry has also mandated the provision of additio nal 50 days of wage employment to every ST \nhousehold in a forest area, provided that these hou seholds have no other private property except \nfor the land rights provided under the Forest Rights Act, 2006. Further, as per Para 5 of Schedule \nI of the Act provides that “Works creating individu al assets shall be prioritized on land or \nhomestead owned by households belonging to the Sche duled Castes and Scheduled tribes. The \nparticipation of SC/ STs Population under Mahatma G andhi NREGA during the last three years \nand current FY 2020-21 (as on 28.01.2021) are as under:  \nIndicator  2017-18  2018-19  2019-20  2020-21*  \nSTs person-days % as of total person-days  17.49  17.42  18.41  17.82  \nSCs personday % as of total persondays  21.56  20.77  19.97  19.84  \n*as on 28.01.2021  \n  \nIt has been decided to make special budgetary provi sion for SC&ST workers' wage payment \nwith effect from FY 2021-22. \n \n \nNo specific case of violation of Mahatma Gandhi NREGS Guidelines related to backward \nclasses people has come to the notice. As the respo nsibility of implementation of Mahatma \nGandhi NREGS is vested with the State Governments/U Ts. Whenever any complaint relating to \nMahatma Gandhi NREGS is received in the Ministry, t he same is forwarded to the concerned \nState Governments/UTs for taking appropriate and ex peditious action as per the provision of \nMahatma Gandhi NREGA.  \n \n*****  \n\n \n \n Annexure-I\n \n \nAnnexure-I referred in reply to part (a) of Lok Sab ha Unstarred Question \nNo.30 dated 02.02.2021 \n \n  \ni. Adult members of a rural household, willing to do u nskilled manual work, may apply for \nregistration in writing or orally to the local Gram Panchayat. \nii. The Gram Panchayat after due verification will issu e a Job Card free of cost within 15 \ndays. \niii. The worker has a right to demand and receive work w ithin 15 days of the receipt of the \napplication or the date of the demand in case of ad vance application, whichever is later, \nas mandated by the Act. \niv. In case employment is not provided within fifteen d ays from the date of registration of \nthe demand for work or the date from which work has  been demanded in case of advance \napplications, whichever is later, the worker is leg ally entitled to a daily unemployment \nallowance.  \nv. Preparation of Labour Budget (LB) which is an essen tial annual work plan document that \nentails planning, approval, funding and project execution modalities.  \nvi. The shelf of projects for a village will be recomme nded by the gram sabha and approved \nby the zilla panchayat. At least 50% of works will be allotted to Gram Panchayats for \nexecution. \nvii. Permissible works predominantly include water and s oil conservation, afforestation and \nland development works. \nviii. A 60:40 wage and material ratio has to be maintaine d at the district level. No contractors \nand labour displacing machinery is allowed. \nix. The Central Government bears 100 percent wage cost of unskilled manual labour and 75 \npercent of the material cost including the wages of skilled and semi-skilled workers. \nx. Social Audit has to be done by the Gram Sabha. \nxi. Grievance redressal mechanisms have to be put in pl ace for ensuring a responsive \nimplementation process. \nxii. All accounts and records relating to the Scheme should be available for public scrutiny. \n  \n  \n\n \n \n \nAnnexure-II \n \nAnnexure-II referred in reply to part (a) of Lok Sa bha Unstarred Question \nNo.30 dated 02.02.2021 \n \n(Rs. in lakh)  \nCentral funds released to Mahatma Gandhi NREGA  \nS. No  States/UTs  2017-18 #  2018-19  2019-\n20                    \n2020-21  \n(as on 28.01.21)  \n1 Andhra Pradesh  512763  657110.67  720472.46  787347.21  \n2 Arunachal Pradesh  20679.65  19800.64  21020.01  27015.07  \n3 Assam  112366.54  103097.37  168752.49  182510.06  \n4 Bihar  246888.44  281936.01  325093.78  552237.25  \n5 Chhattisgarh  289885.21  289476.07  264031.92  315141.38  \n6 Gujarat  82505.07  102449.67  74758.23  121487.28  \n7 Haryana  30140.64  34829.1  33812.58  58292.44  \n8 Himachal Pradesh  58684.46  75090.72  59748.12  89798.49  \n9 Jammu and Kashmir  125417.69  79334.12  118370.31  85150.78  \n10  Jharkhand  135264.57  149989.63  127292.89  256487.55  \n11  Karnataka  295632.54  297813.1  546271.17  485934.09  \n12  Kerala  185406.4  234048.78  352016.95  333909.11  \n13  Madhya Pradesh  376889.92  452909.74  471894.15  737358.86  \n14  Maharashtra  185828.74  194892.31  167066.42  123455.85  \n15  Manipur  15778.89  28698.66  61074.66  73756.92  \n16  Meghalaya  87060.44  78903.38  102443.59  111543.64  \n17  Mizoram  20081.04  39783.58  52507.57  53983.09  \n18  Nagaland  110492.88  19322.98  33044.7  48382.14  \n19  Odisha  219834.66  216328.3  243278.38  443369.44  \n20  Punjab  61895.86  57826.49  74886.26  98403.99  \n21  Rajasthan  472828.41  524471.1  726747.87  732133.73  \n22  Sikkim  10571.15  9586.17  8269.34  8786.70  \n23  Tamil Nadu  583166.13  488286.16  555969.31  726375.12  \n24  Telangana  253920.33  295817.48  222132.13  320024.69 