    "ls|LS17_S5_U_31": "Government of India \nMinistry of Development of North Eastern Region \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *31 \nTo be answered on  \nWednesday, February 3, 2021/Magha 14, 1942 (Saka) \nQUESTION  \nPromoting Entrepreneurship in NER \n*31. SHRIMATI MEENAKSHI LEKHI: \nWill the Minister of DEVLEOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN REGION be pleased \nto state: \n(a) Whether the Government is taking steps to promo te entrepreneurship in the \nNorth Eastern Region (NER); \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government is planning to market th e products being produced in \nthe North Eastern Region through e-commerce platform; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nThe Minister of State (Independent Charge) of the Ministry of Development of \nNorth Eastern Region \n[Dr. Jitendra Singh] \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the Table of the  House.  \n \n***** \n\nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha Starred Question \nNo. *31 for answer on 03.02.2021. \n(a) and (b): Yes Sir. The Government has initiated and implemented several initiatives \nfor promotion of entrepreneurship and for improveme nt in enabling environment and \nvibrant ecosystem to enable entrepreneurs to tap th e potential of the region. Important \nconnectivity projects for capital rail, air and roa d connectivity will be completed in the \nnext two to three years. Development of Inland Wate rways- National Waterways 2 and \nNational Waterways 16  will provide connectivity to  Haldia through Indo-Bangladesh \nProtocol Route and greatly benefit the eco-system by reducing cost. \n2. Bamboo which has the potential to change the NE economy has got a boost with \nsetting up of bio-refinery at Numaligarh, Assam; Bamboo Industrial Park at Dima Hasao, \nAssam; with reduced imports following enhancement o f import duty to 25% on  bamboo  \nsticks for Agarbattis and amendment in Indian Forest Act, 1927.  \n3. The institutional framework for promotion of ent repreneurship is provided by \nseveral institutes in NER like Indian Institute of Entrpreneurship (IIE), Guwahati; three \nIndian Institutes of Information Technology(IIIT) i n NER,  Institutes of Hotel \nManagement at Guwahati and Shillong, Film and Telev ision Institute at Jollang-Rakap \n(Jote), Arunachal Pradesh, and National Institute o f Design (NID), Jorhat, Assam.  \nThese institutes provide opportunity for skilling a nd nurturing young creative talent and \ndesign aspirants from North East Region and boost e ntrepreneurship.   Further, Krishi \nUdaan Scheme to evacuate local produce, 586 Van Dha n Vikas Kendras and Zoram \nMega Food Park etc. will boost entrepreneurship, tr ade and commerce.  In Assam and \nMeghalaya, the PMYUVA Pilot is being implemented in  selected Industrial Training \nInstitutes (ITIs), Polytechnics, Pradhan Mantri Kau shal Kendra (PMKKs) centres and \nJan Sikshan Sansthan (JSS).  Under PMYUVA, 25 project institutes have been selected \nfor the pilot project on Entrepreneurship Development in Assam and Meghalaya.   \n4. Entrepreneurs in NER have also been benefitted u nder various credit related \nschemes. Under Stand Up India Scheme, 3,449 loan ac counts of SC/ST/Women \nbeneficiaries were sanctioned for Rs. 714.83 crore and under Pradhan Mantri Mudra \nYojana, a total of 1,07,26,209 loan accounts were s anctioned for Rs 48,868 crore in the \n8 North Eastern States.  Under Prime Minister’s Emp loyment Generation Programme \n24,455 units  were set up and employment for 1,95,6 40 persons was generated  from \n\n2017-18 to 31.12.2020; under Credit Guarantee Trust  Fund of Micro and Small \nEnterprises (CGTMSE), an amount of Rs.4684.55 crore  was approved towards 98569 \nguarantees.  An amount of Rs.21.26 crore was settle d for 83393   beneficiaries under \nInterest Subvention Scheme for Incremental Credit t o MSME since its inception in \nNovember, 2018.                                                                                                                                                                                                              \n5. The Start-up India Initiatives aims at fostering  entrepreneurship and promoting \ninnovation by creating and enabling eco-system. Und er this, 408 startups have been \nrecognized in the NER.   \n \n \n6. North East Development Finance Institution(NEDFI) h as extended financial \nassistance to 6844 no. of projects with total sanct ions and disbursement of Rs.5487.92 \nCr. & Rs.4222.64 Cr. respectively, facilitating cap ital formation of around Rs.15922 Cr. \nduring 1995 to 2020.The North East Venture Fund (NE VF), the first dedicated Venture \nFund for NE has approved investments worth Rs 59.52  Crore in 29 ventures across \nsectors including healthcare, mobility, IT &ITeS, f ood processing/food tech, adventure \ntourism, agri-allied services and handicraft.  \n7. Under North East Industrial Development Scheme  (NEIDS), 2017,  202 units \nhave been registered.    \n \n(c) and (d): Organizations such as  North East Hand icrafts & Handloom  Corporation \n(NEHHDC),Tribal Cooperative Marketing Development F ederation of India (TRIFED), \nKhadi and Village Industries Commission(KVIC) have launched E- Commerce portals  \ni.e. \nwww.purbashree.com,  www.tribesindia.com, ekhadiindia.com which enable  \nentrepreneurs including from North East to reach ou t to a wider market.   The \norganizations like NEHHDC and TRIFED are also suppo rting the marketing of NE \nproducts through other e-commerce platforms.   \n \n*****  \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S5_U_32": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.32 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2021 \n \nCONSTRUCTION OF FOBS AND UNDER BRIDGES \n \n \n*32. SHRI MANOJ KOTAK: \n              \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the number of Foot Over Bridges (FOBs) and Under Bridges \nproposed to be constructed by Railways in the Central Railway Zone, \nlocation-wise, especially in Mumbai; \n \n(b) whether any target has been fixed for completing the construction \nof all proposed FOBs and Under Bridges; and \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \n \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMERCE & INDUSTRY AND  \nCONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n(a) to (c) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n***** \n  \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.32 BY SHRI MANOJ KOTAK TO BE \nANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 03.02.2021 REGARDING \nCONSTRUCTION OF FOBS AND UNDER BRIDGES \n \n(a) to (c) Total 44 number of Foot Over Bridges (FOBs) are \nproposed to be con structed on Central Railway within next 03 \nyears. In Mumbai area, total 40 Nos. of FOBs (10 in Central \nRailway and 30 in Western Railway) are proposed to be \nconstructed, out of which 19 FOBs (03 in Central Railway and 16 \nin Western Railway) have already be en constructed and \nremaining 21 FOBs are targeted to be completed in next two \nyears.  \nThe details of sanctioned Foot Over Bridges (location -wise) \nunder Central Railway and Mumbai area of Western Railway are \nat Appendix -I.  \nTotal 255 Nos. of Road Under Brid ges (RUBs) are proposed to be \nconstructed on Central Railway. In Mumbai area, total 22 \nnumbers of RUBs in Central Railway are proposed to be \nconstructed, out of which 01 RUB has already been constructed. \nTarget of completion of RUBs depends on many factors  like \ncatchment area/extent of drainage improvement works in State \nGovernment portion, land acquisition, encroachment, if any, on \nthe approaches etc.  However, progress of works is \ncommensurate with availability of funds.  \nThe details of Road Under Bridges  in Mumbai area under Central \nRailway are at Appendix -II. \n***** \n \n \n\n \n \nAPPENDIX-I REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (c) OF STARRED \nQUESTION  NO. 32 BY SHRI MANOJ KOTAK TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK \nSABHA ON 03.02.2021 REGARDING CONSTRUCTION OF FOBs AND \nUNDER BRIDGES \n(i) The details of location -wise Foot Over Bridges proposed to be \nconstructed on Central Railway are as under:- \nDivision \nNos of \nFOBs \nsanctioned \nLocations \nMumbai 10 \nAlready completed  (3):  Titwala, Dombivali \nand Thakurli \n \nUnder Construction (7):  Khadavali, Vasind, \nAtgaon, Ambernath, Palasdari, Kurla (KYN \nend) & Rohe. \nBhusawal 18 \nJalgaon, Murtizapur, Badnera, Nandura, \nBodwad, Amravati, New Amravati, Nagzhri, \nChandur Bazar, Malkapur, Akola, Bhusawal, \nDevlali, Ghoti, Chalisgaon, Nandgaon, \nJalgaon & Nasik Road. \nNagpur 4 Nagpur, Ballarshah, Wardha & Amla. \nPune 3 Kadethan, Chinchwad & Patas \nSolapur 3 Solapur, Daund & Barshi Town. \nRVNL 6 \nBablad, Boroti, Gaudgaon, Gangapur Road, \nKulali & Savalgi. \nTOTAL 44  \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n(ii)  The details of location-wise Foot Over Bridges proposed to be \nconstructed in Mumbai area on Western Railway are as under: \n \nDivision \nNo. of \nSanctioned \nFOBs \nLocations \nMumbai  30 \nAlready Completed (16) - Marine Lines \n(North), Andheri (South), CCG -MEL (Hindi \nVidya Bhavan), Vedchha, Maroli, Palghar \n(New), Palghar (Extension), Bandra (Middle), \nAndheri(North) , Dadar (South) MCGM, Malad \n(South) , BDTS (South), Grant Road(South), \nMumbai Central (South), JOG -GM midsection \nand Vasai Road (Middle) \n \nWork in Progress (14) -  Marine Lines (South), \nPoisar Nala, Naigaon, Mahim (North) MCGM, \nSantacruz (South) MCGM, Bandra (South), \nKhar (South)MCGM, Bandra (North), Dahisar \n(North),  Bhayandar (South), Vasai Road \n(South), Khar -Bandra Midsection, Santacruz \n(south) and Ville Parle (Middle). \n \n*****  \n\n \nAPPENDIX-II REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (c) OF STARRED \nQUESTION  NO. 32 BY SHRI MANOJ KOTAK TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK \nSABHA ON 03.02.2021 REGARDING CONSTRUCTION OF FOBs AND \nUNDER BRIDGES \n(i) The details of construction of Road Under Bridges in Mumbai area \nunder Central Railway are as under:- \n \nS.No. \nLevel  \nCrossing \nNo. \nName of work \n1 10 \nAlready completed: Diva-Panvel RUB in lieu of LC \nNo.10 at Kms.57/5-6. \nLevel Crossing closed on Nov,2020.  \n2 17 \nDiwa-Roha: Construction of RUB in lieu of LC gate No. \n17(M) at Kms. 105/9-106/0. \n3 35 \nDiwa-Roha: Construction of RUB in lieu of LC gate No. \n35(M) at Kms. 119/0-1. \n4 53 \nElimination of manned LC No.53 at Kms. 67/6 -7 on \nbetween stations TLA-ADV ON  KYN-IGP SECTION. \n5 54 \nElimination of manned LC No.54 at Kms. 68/4 -5 on \nbetween stations TLA-ADV ON KYN-IGP SECTION.  \n6 58 \nElimination of manned LC No.58 at Kms. 72/4 -5 on \nbetween stations KDV-VSD on KYN-IGP Section. \n7 59 \nElimination of manned LC No. 59 at Kms. 74/11 -12 on \nbetween stations KDV-VSD on KYN-IGP Section. \n8 64 \nElimination of manned LC No. 64 at Kms. 80/12 -13 on \nbetween stations VSD-ASO Section. \n9 67 \nElimination of manned LC No. 67 at Kms. 87/6 -7 on \nbetween stations ASO-ATG on KYN-IGP Section. \n10 76 \nElimination of manned LC No. 76 at Kms. 117/11 -12 on \nbetween stations OMB-KSRA on KYN-IGP Section. \n11 3 \nPNVL-Roha: Provision of Subway by providing RCC Box \nby pushing method in lieu of LC Gate No. 3 at Km 78/6-\n7 in Panvel Roha Section. \n\n12 4 \nPNVL-Roha: Provision of Subway by providing RCC Box \nby pushing method in lieu of LC Gate No. 4 at Km 79/7-\n8 in Panvel Roha Section. \n13 6 \nPNVL-Roha: Provision of Subway by providing RCC Box \nby pushing method in lieu of LC Gate No. 6 at Km 82/4-\n5 in Panvel Roha Section. \n14 23 \nPNVL-Roha: Provision of Subway by providing RCC Box \nby pushing method in lieu of LC Gate No. 23 at Km \n109/1 in Panvel Roha Section. \n15 24 \nPNVL-Roha: Provision of Subway by providing RCC Box \nby pushing method in lieu of LC Gate No. 24 at Km \n109/5-6 in Panvel Roha Section. \n16 30 \nPNVL-Roha: Provision of Subway by providing RCC Box \nby pushing method in lieu of LC Gate No. 30 at Km \n114/6-7 in Panvel Roha Section. \n17 60 \nKYN-IGP : Proposed construction work of RUB by Box \npushing method at km 76/11-12 in lieu of level crossing \nNo. 60 in KYN-IGP section. \n18 61 \nKYN-IGP : Proposed construction work of RUB by Box \npushing method at km 77/17-18 in lieu of level crossing \nNo. 61 in KYN-IGP section. \n19 68 \nKYN-IGP : Proposed construction work of RUB by Box \npushing method at km 89.460 in lieu of level crossing \nNo. 68 in KYN-IGP section. \n20 23 \nPanvel-Jasai : RUB in lieu of LC No. 23 at kms 73/10-\n11. \n21 2 \nDiva-Panvel: RUB in lieu of LC No. 2 at km 47/7-8 \n(DFCCIL) \n22 For \ntrespassing \nMid Section: RUB at km 35/41 of Thane-Airoli section.  \nThis RUB is being constructed as trespassing \nmeasures by MRVC. \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S5_U_33": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 33 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2021 \n \nKHAMGAON �� JALNA RAILWAY LINE \n \n����33.   SHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV:  \n \n  Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has received requests from the \npublic-representatives in the month of January, 2021 for laying \nof Khamgaon -Jalna railway line via Shegaon in place of \nJalamb;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) whether the Government has taken or proposes to take any \naction in this regard; and  \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof including the current status in this \nregard? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMERCE & INDUSTRY AND \nCONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n \n(SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n \n(a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n***** \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 33 BY SHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV  TO BE \nANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 03.02.2021 REGARDING KHAMGAON �� \nJALNA RAILWAY LINE \n \n \n(a) to (d):    Yes, Sir.  Request has been received to consider \nextension of Jalna-Khamgaon (162 km) new line survey to Shegaon in \nplace of Jalamb.  Both the stations Jala mb and Shegaon exist on \nBhusaval-Wardha Broad Gauge line. Since Shegaon is already \nconnected to Khamgaon via Jalamb (19 km), currently there is no \nproposal for direct survey from Khamgaon to Shegaon (17.5 km). \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S5_U_34": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n34\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 2\nND\n \nFEBRUARY, 2021\n \n \nACCIDENTS IN AGRICULTURE SECTOR\n \n \n34. \n \nSHRI PRATHAP SIMHA: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE   \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state: \n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether\n \nthere is any scheme for providing compensation of insurance claim to the \nvictims of accidents in the agriculture sector; \n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details thereof; and \n \n \n(c) \n \nthe number of such victims provided compensation during the last two years and the \ncurre\nnt year?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n   \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n(a) to (c)\n: \n \nGovernment has introduced Pradhan Mantri Suraksha\n \nBima Yojana (PMSBY) \nto cover personal accidents of people including people working in rural/agriculture sector in \nthe age group of 18\n-\n70 years having bank/post office account and who give their consent to \njoin/enable auto\n-\ndebit on or before 31\nst\n \nMay for t\nhe coverage period from 1\nst\n \nJuly to 31\nst\n \nMay \non an annual renewable basis.   The risk coverage under the scheme is Rs. 2 lakh for \naccidental death and full disability and Rs. 1 lakh for partial disability. The premium of Rs. 12 \nper annum is to be deducted \nfrom the account holder’s bank / Post office account through \n‘auto\n-\ndebit’ facility. The scheme is being offered by Public Sector General Insurance \nCompanies or any other General Insurance Company who is offering the product on similar \nterms with necessary \napprovals and tie up with Banks and Post Offices for this purpose.\n \nDetails of claims paid under PMSBY are given below :\n \n \nCumulative Number of Claims Paid since Inception of PMSBY May, \n2015 (Financial Year\n-\nwise)\n \nPeriod\n \nCumulative No. of Claims Paid\n \nAs on \nMarch, 2019\n \n32176\n \nAs on March, 2020\n \n39969\n \nAs on December, 2020\n \n43617\n \nContd….2/\n-\n \n\n-\n \n2 \n-\n \n \n \nF\narmers can also insure themselves under personal accident\n \npolicy under Kisan \nCredit Card Scheme upto the age of 70 years for a risk cover of Rs. 50,000/\n-\n \nfor accidental \ndeath or total disability and Rs. 25,000/\n-\n \nfor partial disability.  Premium rate is Rs. 15 per year \nand some portion of the premium is borne by t\nhe concerned bank. \n \n \nFurther, t\no cater to all insurance needs of farmers a Unified Package Insurance \nScheme (UPIS) has been introduced from Kharif 2016 as a pilot scheme.  The scheme is \nvoluntary for the State Governments to implement the pilot in the sele\ncted districts. The pilot \nscheme has been designed to take care of all the insurance needs of farmers associated \nwith their life, properties and agricultural activities such as crops, \nloss of life, accidental death \n& disability, student safety, household, \nagriculture implements and tractor.  Out of total of 7 \ncovers under UPIS, Crop Insurance is compulsory and the farmer has to avail \natleast\n \ntwo \nfrom the remaining six covers.  Insured farmers have to also furnish the details, if they \nalready availed similar\n \ncoverage for them or their family members.    Most of the States had \nmade coverage of these two sections voluntary; therefore, the coverage under other sections \nof the package including personal accident except crop insurance has been negligible or not \nre\nported.   \n \n \n*******\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S5_U_35": "1 \n \n15th Position \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COAL \n  \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 35 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2021 \n  \n  \nOpening of new Coal Mines \n  \n  \n*35.     DR. SANJEEV KUMAR SINGARI: \n  \n  \n    Will the MINISTER OF COAL be pleased to state: \n  \n(a)  whether the  Government  is  planning  to  open new coal mines; \n  \n(b) if so, the details of the proposed number of new coal mines and the \nproposed sites of mining; \n  \n(c)  whether  the  Government  is  planning  to  use forest lands for the \nproposed new mines and if so, the details of place and number of hectares of \nforest land to be used in case of each new mine; and \n  \n(d) the percentage of energy needs of India that are achieved through coal \nmining? \n  \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS, COAL AND MINES \n(SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI) \n  \n(a) to (d):      A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n**** \n \n \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n\n2 \n \n  \n15th Position \n   \nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha Starred \nQuestion No.35 for answer on 03.02.2021 asked by Dr. Sanjeev Kumar \nSingari regarding 'Opening of new Coal Mines'. \n  \n(a) Yes Sir. \n(b) & (c): Coal India Ltd. (CIL) and its subsidiaries have planned to open 22 new \ncoal mines. The details are as under: \nSl. \nNo. \nSubsidiary Name of Mine State Area of \nForest \nLand (Ha) \n1 Eastern Coalfields \nLtd. (ECL) \nHura C OC Jharkhand 560 \n2 Bharat Coking Coal \nLtd. (BCCL) \nMoonidih XV Seam \nUG \nJharkhand Nil \n3 Central Coalfields \nLtd. (CCL) \nSanghmitra OC Jharkhand 553.53 \n4 CCL Chandragupta OC Jharkhand 663.90 \n5 CCL Patratu ABC UG Jharkhand 103.43 \n6 CCL Piparwar Ph-I UG Jharkhand 37.81 \n7 CCL Kotre Basantpur \nPachmo OC \nJharkhand 814.93 \n8 CCL Pichri OC Jharkhand 23.02 \n9 Northern Coalfields \nLtd. (NCL) \nSemaria OC Madhya Pradesh 333.16 \n10 Western Coalfields \nLtd. (WCL) \nGandhigram UG Madhya Pradesh 3.28 \n11 WCL Sharda UG Madhya Pradesh 9.5 \n12 WCL Tawa III UG Madhya Pradesh 101.60 \n13 WCL Dhankasa UG Madhya Pradesh 385.84 \n14 South Eastern \nCoalfields Ltd. (SECL) \nAmbika OC Chhattisgarh 6.28 \n15 SECL Rampur Batura OC Madhya Pradesh 4.65 \n16 SECL Durgapur OC Chhattisgarh 251.84 \n17 SECL Ketki UG Chhattisgarh 208 \n18 SECL Madannagar OC Chhattisgarh 982.60 \n19 SECL Pelma OC Chhattisgarh 390.73 \n20 SECL Porda Chimtapani Chhattisgarh 183.03 \n21 SECL Batura West OC Madhya Pradesh Nil \n22 Mahanadi Coalfields \nLtd. (MCL) \nSiarmal OCP Odisha 349.71 \n\n3 \n \n  \n          Further the following 19 coal mines have been recently auctioned for \ncommercial mining under the provisions of Coal Mines (Special Provisions) Act, \n2015 and the rules made thereunder as well as under the provisions of Mines \nand Minerals (Development and Regulation) Act, 1957 and the rules made \nthereunder: \nSl. \nNo. \nBlock Successful Bid\nder \nArea (sq.\nkm.) \nTentatively Es\ntimated Green \nCover from For\nest Survey of I\nndia Map (%) \nForest Area (i\nn Ha) as per \nMining Plan s\nubmitted by \nprior allottee \n1 Bandha EMIL Mines an\nd Mineral Reso\nurces Ltd. \n18.5 40 Not Available \n2 Brahmadiha The Andhra Pra\ndesh Mineral D\nevelopment Cor\nporation Ltd. \n0.87 -- Nil \n3 Chakla Hindalco Indust\nries Limited \n8.63 -- 550 \n4 Dhirauli Stratatech Mine\nral Resources P\nrivate Ltd. \n26.72 55 Not Available \n5 Gare Palma IV\n/1 \nJindal Power Li\nmited \n10.15 -- 91.179 \n6 Gare Palma IV\n/7 \nSarda Energy a\nnd Minerals Ltd\n. \n4.82 5 Nil \n7 Gondulpara Adani Enterpris\nes Limited \n4.1 -- 176 \n8 Gotitoria East Boulder Stone \nMart Private Lt\nd. \n1.12 -- 98.85 \n9 Gotitoria West 2.04 -- \n10 Marki Mangli II Yazdani Interna\ntional Private Lt\nd. \n3.4 -- 111.8 \n11 Radhikapur Ea\nst \nEMIL Mines an\nd Mineral Reso\nurces Ltd. \n10.16 -- 218.9 \n12 Radhikapur W\nest \nVedanta Ltd. 10.29 -- 84.7949 \n  \n\n4 \n \n13 Rajhara North (\nCentral & East\nern) \nFairmine Carbo\nns Private Ltd. \n1.09 -- Nil \n14 Sahapur East Chowgule and \nCompany Privat\ne Ltd. \n6.59 -- 9.199 \n15 Sahapur West Sarda Energy a\nnd Minerals Ltd\n. \n6.19 -- 35.263 \n16 Takli Jena Bell\nora North & So\nuth \nAurobindo Reali\nty and Infrastru\ncture Private Lt\nd. \n9.36 0 -- \n17 Urma Pharitola 9.76 15 Not Available \n18 Urtan JMS Mining Pri\nvate Ltd. \n3.79 0 Not Available \n19 Urtan North 4.77 -- Nil \n  \n(d): Currently, coal accounts for approximately 55% of the country’s primary \nenergy consumption and CIL accounts for about 83% of country’s coal \nproduction. \n****  \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S5_U_36": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n36\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 2\nND\n \nFEBRUARY, 2021\n \n \nPADDY CROP\n \n \n36. \n \nSHRI G.S. BASAVARAJ: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE   \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state: \n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether it is a fact that paddy farmers from Punjab have sold out record bumper crop \nof paddy at MSP of Rs. 1,988/\n-\n \nper quintal for the 2020\n-\n21 season; and \n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details thereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n   \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n(a) & (b)\n:\n \n \nThe Minimum Support Price (MSP) of paddy fixed by Government of India for \nKharif Marketing Season 2020\n-\n21 is Rs. 1868/qtl for Paddy Common and Rs. 1888/qtl. for \nPaddy Grade ‘A’ variety. During \nKharif Marketing Season 2020\n-\n21, a record quantity of \n202.77 LMT paddy has been procured in Punjab at MSP fixed by Government of India \nbenefitting 14.89 lakh farmers and Rs. 38,284 Crore paid towards MSP to the farmers of \nPunjab.\n \n \n*******\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S5_U_37": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.37 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2021 \n \nGARIB KALYAN ROZGAR ABHIYAAN \n*37.  DR. PRITAM GOPINATHRAO MUNDE: \n   \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government had announced that an amount of Rs \n50,000 crore would be spent for building durable rural infrastructure \nunder the Garib Kalyan Rozgar Abhiyaan to provide jobs to migrant \nworkers who had returned home during COVID-19 lockdown; \n \n(b)  if so, whether the Indian Railways has generated more than \n5.5 lakhs mandays of work under the Garib Kalyan Rozgar Abhiyaan \nin selected States; \n(c)  if so, the details thereof; \n(d) whether the Indian Railways has identified infrastructure \nprojects which are to be developed under Garib Kalyan Rozgar \nAbhiyaan and if so, the details thereof; and \n(e)  the number of migrated workers who have been engaged till \ndate under the Garib Kalyan Rozgar Abhiyaan in the Indian \nRailways?  \n  \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMERCE & INDUSTRY AND   \nCONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION  \n  \n(SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n  \n(a) to (e):   A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.                                                     \n \n****** \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.37 BY DR. PRITAM GOPINATHRAO MUNDE \nTO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 03.02.2021 REGARDING GARIB \nKALYAN ROZGAR ABHIYAAN \n \n(a)  Yes, Sir.  \n \n(b) & (c) Yes, Sir.  The Garib Kalyan Rozgar Abhiyaan was announced \non 20.06.2020 for 125 days i.e. up to 22.10.2020. The Indian \nRailways generated 14,14,604 mandays during Garib  Kalyan Rozgar \nAbhiyaan in six selected States. Details are as under: \nS.No. State No. of mandays generated \n1 Bihar 3,95,527 \n2 Jharkhand 20,572 \n3 Madhya Pradesh 2,05,415 \n4 Odisha 84,190 \n5 Rajasthan 1,96,521 \n6 Uttar Pradesh 5,12,379 \n Total 14,14,604 \n  \n \n…2/- \n \n\n-2- \n(d) Yes, Sir.  The Indian Railways identified 143 No. of infrastructure \nprojects like doubling of railway line, electrification of line, \nconstruction of new line,  construction of Bridge/Road over Bridge, \nSignaling works etc. under Garib Kalyan Rozgar Abhiyaan in six \nselected States. Details are as under: \nS.No. State No. of infrastructure \nprojects \n1 Bihar 36 \n2 Jharkhand 4 \n3 Madhya Pradesh 28 \n4 Odisha 6 \n5 Rajasthan 16 \n6 Uttar Pradesh 53 \n Total 143 \n \n(e) No separate data was maintained for engagement of migrated \nworkers during the Garib Kalyan Rozgar Abhiyaan. However, total \n14,14,604 mandays were created by Railways in six selected States \nduring the Abhiyaan period from 20.06.2020 to 22.10.2020 (125 days).  \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S5_U_38": "                                                   GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.38 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2021 \n \nHOSPITAL MANAGEMENT INFORMATION SYSTEM \n \n       *38. DR. BEESETTI VENKATA SATYAVATHI:  \n               SHRI ADALA PRABHAKARA REDDY: \n \n           Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Railways has launched Hospital Management \nInformation System as a trial project;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) whether th is facility is available only for the employees of the \nIndian Railways and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMERCE & INDUSTRY AND  \nCONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n \n(SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n(a) to (c) : A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*****  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.38 BY DR. BEESETTI VENKATA SATYAVATHI \nAND SHRI ADALA PRABHAKARA REDDY TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK \nSABHA ON 03.02.2021 REGARDIN G HOSPITAL MANAGEMENT \nINFORMATION SYSTEM \n \n(a)  & (b): Yes, Sir. A Pilot Project of Hospital Management Information \nSystem (HMIS) has been launched on 28.12.2020 inter -linking Northern \nRailway Hospitals located in Delhi.  The Pilot Project is helping in \nbetter healthcare management of railway medical beneficiaries. \n  \n(c) Yes, Sir. The facility is for railway employees and their eligible \ndependents only.  \n****** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S5_U_39": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No.39 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2021 \n \nDALLI RAJHARA-JAGDALPUR-RAWGHAT RAILWAY LINE  \n \n†*39. SHRI DEEPAK BAIJ: :   \n \n Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) the present status of under construction Dalli Rajhara -\nJagdalpur-Rawghat railway line under East Coast Railway;  \n(b) the time when the construction work of the said railway line \nhad started;  \n(c) the length of the railway line in kilometres constructed so far \nalong with the length of railway line still to be constructed;  \n(d) whether the construction work of this railway line is going at \na very slow pace and the people have been deprived of train \nfacility as a result thereof; and  \n(e) the initial cost o f this project along with its present cost     \nwith cost overrun and the factors responsible for the same? \nANSWER \n                  MINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMERCE & INDUSTRY AND   \n                 CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n  \n(SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n(a) to (e) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.   \n***** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO ( e) OF \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.39  BY SHRI DEEPAK BAIJ  TO BE \nANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 03.02.2021 REGARDING DALLI \nRAJHARA-JAGDALPUR-RAWGHAT RAILWAY LINE  \n \n(a) to (c)  Railway has taken up construction of Dallirajhara -\nRowghat-Jagdalpur rail line project which was included in budget \n1995-96 . A Memorandum of Under standing for sharing the cost \nwith the Ministry of Steel and the State Government of Madhya \nPradesh was finalized on 2nd April 1998.  Project has been taken u p \nin two phases. Phase-I –Dallirajhara-Rowghat (0- 95 km) and Phase-\nII-Rowghat-Jagdalpur(95- 235 k m). Phase I between Dallirajhara -\nRowghat (95km) is being executed by RVNL through funding by \nSAIL (Steel Authority of India Limited). Phase II between Rowghat – \nJagdalpur (140 km) has been planned for execution through SPV \nnamed Bastar Railway Private Limi ted ( BRPL) consisting of \nGovernment of Ch hattisgarh, SAIL, National Mineral Development \nCorporation (NMDC) and IRCON.   The present status of the project \nis as under:  \n(1) Work on Phase-I Dallirajhara -Rowghat (95 Km)  portion was \nstarted in 2010.  The work from Dallirajhara to Antagarh (60 Km) \nhas been completed. 35 km work is balance between Antagarh to \nRowghat.  \n(2) Phase-II Rowghat-Jagdalpur (140 Km) is being executed by \nIRCON through Special Purpose Vehicle(SPV), n amed Bastar \nRailway Private Limited (BRPL)  . Land acquisition and forest land \ndiversion for this phase is in process.  Financial closure of project \nis in process.                                                                      ……..2/- \n \n\n: 2 : \nd) The completion of any Railway project depends on various \nfactors like quick land acquisition by State Government, forest \nclearance by forest department, statutory clearances from various \nauthorities, geological and topographical conditions of area, law \nand order situation in the area of project site, number of working \nmonths in a year due to climatic considerations, cooperation of all \nparties for early completion of project  etc. All these factors affect \nthe completion time and cost of the project. \ne) The  initial MoU for execution of the project was signed on 2 nd \nApril 1998 amongst Ministry of Railways, Government of Madhya \nPradesh, Steel Authority of India Limited and National Mineral \nDevelopment Corporation. However the project could not be taken \nup for want of clearance under Forest Conservation Act, 1980 for \nthe Rowghat Mining Projects of SAIL. Revised MoU was signed on \n11th December 2007 amongst Ministry of Railways, Government of \nChhattisgarh, Steel Authority of India Limited and National Mineral \nDevelopment Corporation . The anticipated cost of the project in \nthis period increased to ₹  968.6 Cr at price level of 2004 -05 from \nabstract cost of ₹  369 Cr in 1995-96.  \nPhase-I- Dallirajhara-Rowghat (95 km) : The abstract cost of the \nproject for Phase I  was ₹  134.96 Cr which increased to ₹  304.3 Cr \nat the time of signing of MoU in Dec 2007. The latest anticipated \ncost of the project is ₹  1622 Cr. Work on this portion was started in \n2010.  The work from Dallirajhara to Antagarh (60 Km) has been  \n……3/- \n\n: 3 : \ncompleted. Work has been taken up between km 60 to Km 80 and \nKm 93 to Km 95, where land is available. Work from km 80 to km 93 \nwill be taken up after handing over of encumbrance free land to \nRailway by Government of Chhattisgarh.  Government of \nChhattisgarh has to handover forest land in 4.76 km length after \ntree cutting (1043 Nos.) and 27.54 ha private land in km 89 -93. \nProject area is LWE ( Left Wing Extremism ) affected and work has \nto be taken up under dedicated Security cover. Daily mobilization \nand demobi lization of man and machinery is required due to \nsecurity reasons. This leaves only few daylight hours for working.  \nNon handing over of complete encumbrance free land by State \nGovernment/Forest department, and law & order problem in the \narea has delayed th e project. The above factors have led to time \nand cost overruns for this project. \nPhase-II- Rowghat-Jagdalpur(140 Km) - The abstract cost of the \nproject for Phase II was ₹ 234.04 Cr and latest anticipated cost of \nthis phase is ₹ 2539 Crore. The project involves acquisition of 320 \nHa private land, 47 Ha. Govt. land and 409 Ha. Forest land diversion \nand land acquisition  is under process. Financial closure of project \nis in process. These issues have led to delay in execution. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S5_U_4": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n \n4\n \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 2\nND\n \nFEBRUARY, 2021\n \n \nDIVERSIFICATION OF CROPS\n \n \n4. \n \nSHRI S. JAGATHRAKSHAKAN: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE   \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \nbe pleased to state: \n \n \n(a)\n \n \nthe measures taken/to be taken by the Government towards educating farmers to \nlook for diversifying crops instead of\n \nthe routine summer and winter crop plantation; and \n \n \n(b) \n \nthe steps taken/to be taken by the Government to spread awareness amongst farmers \nabout farming, harvesting, packaging and selling, vital for society?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS \nWELFARE\n \n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n   \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n(a) \n& (b)\n:\n \nThe Department of Agriculture, Cooperation & Farmers Welfare (DAC&FW) has \nbeen encouraging diversified production of crops such as rice, wheat, pulses, coarse cereals, \nnutri\n \ncereals, commercial crops & oilseeds under National Food Security Mission (NFSM). \nHorticultural crops are promoted under Mission for Integrated Development of Horticulture \n(MIDH). In addition, \nCrop Diversification Programme (CDP), a sub scheme of Rashtriy\na \nKrishi Vikas Yojana (RKVY), is being implemented in original green revolution states viz. \nPunjab, Haryana and western Uttar Pradesh to diversify paddy area towards alternative \ncrops and shifting of tobacco \nfarming to\n \nalternative crops/cropping system in \ntobacco growing \nstates.\n \n \n             \nIndian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR) conduct\ns\n \nextensive research and \ntechnology validation through farmers’ participatory research on cropping systems under \nAICRP\n-\nIntegrated Farming Systems (AICRP\n-\nIFS) in 25 \nStates/Union Territory with the \nparticipation of 34 State Agricultural Universities, 6 ICAR Institutes and 1 Central University. \nTraining of farmers and awareness on crop diversification is also undertaken through on\n-\nfarm \nresearch centres in 32 districts o\nf 21 States under the scheme. The studies carried out under \nthe aegis of AICRP\n-\nIFS at research farms of 37 centres, representing different agro\n-\nclimatic \nregions of the country, during past two decades have indicated that under assured irrigated \nconditions \nthere is ample scope to include pulses, vegetables, oilseeds and other high value \ncrops.  \n \nContd….2/\n-\n \n\n \n-\n \n2 \n-\n \n \n \nThe farmers are educated on latest crop production technologies, harvesting, \nmarketing etc to choose appropriate crops/cropping system through \ndemonstrations at \nfarmers field, f\narmer field school,\n \ntraining, kisan melas, conferences, seminars and \nelectronic \nmass media. Beside, crop related advisories are sent to the registered farmers through \nShort \nMessage Service (SMS)\n \non mKisan Portal. \nInformati\non on weather, market, various farm \noperations, outbreak of pest and disease incidence and their control measures are also given \nto farmers through SMS.\n \n \n           \nFurther, DAC&FW has launched Agriculture Infrastructure Fund (AIF), a Central \nSector Scheme\n, under Aatmanirbhar Bharat Abhiyan for creation of infrastructure at the farm \ngate through investment in viable projects for post\n-\nharvest management infrastructure and \ncommunity farming assets through interest subvention and financial support.\n \n \n*********\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S5_U_40": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n40\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 2\nND\n \nFEBRUARY, 2021\n \n \nORGANISATIONAL STRUCTURE OF AGRICULTURE MINISTRY\n \n \n40. \n \nSHRI P.P. CHAUDHARY: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE   \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state: \n \n \n(a)\n \nwhether the Government is considering to change the organizational structure of the \nMinistry of Agriculture; \n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details thereof;. \n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the organizational structural change will affect the budget of the Ministry and \nif so, the details thereof; \n \n \n(d) \n \nwhether any study was undertaken or any other process undertaken to bring the \nchange; and\n \n \n \n(e\n) \n \nif so, the details thereof including process, results and people involved?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n   \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n(a)\n:\n \nThe\n \nchange \nin\n \norganizational structure of Department of Agriculture, Cooperation and \nFarmers Welfare, Ministry of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare\n \nhas not yet been finalized.\n \n \n(b) to (e)\n:\n \nQuestion does not arise.\n \n \n********\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S5_U_41": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 41 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 02ND FEBRUARY, 2021/ MAGHA 13, 1942 (SAKA) \n \nUSE OF CCTNS \n \n41. SHRI RANJEETSINGH HINDURAO NAIK NIMBALKAR: \nMS. PRATIMA BHOUMIK: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is giving importance of Crime and Criminal \nTracking Network & Systems (CCTNS)/ Interoperable Criminal Justice \nSystem (ICJS) and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether it is a fact that many police stations are still not connected with \nCCTNS Software, if so the details thereof and the action taken by the \nGovernment in this regard; \n \n(c) the number of courts using CCTNS data, State and UT-wise; and \n \n(d) the number of courts doing reverse transfer of court data into CCTNS, \nState and UT-wise? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n(a):  Yes, sir. Use of Crime and Criminal Tracking Networks and Systems \n(CCTNS) and Inter-Operable Criminal Justice System (ICJS) is regularly \nreviewed by the Government with the States/ Union Territories. Further, the \nGovernment has leveraged the CCTNS and ICJS to launch a series of Smart \nPolicing initiatives for States/ Union Territories.  \n \n\n--2-- \nL.S.US.Q.NO. 41 FOR 02.02.2021 \n(b): Against an original target of 14306, the CCTNS has been deployed at \n15773 police stations in the country (including new police stations). \nHowever, CCTNS software has not been implemented in 383 police stations. \nDeployment has commenced.  \n \n(c) & (d) ICJS provides access of CCTNS data to eCourts users and similarly \nof eCourts data to CCTNS users. However, specific details on use are not \nmaintained centrally.  \n**** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S5_U_42": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 42 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 02ND FEBRUARY, 2021/ MAGHA 13, 1942 (SAKA) \n \nFORCED RELIGIOUS CONVERSION \n \n42. DR. MOHAMMAD JAWED: \nSHRI ANTO ANTONY: \nSHRI T.N. PRATHAPAN: \nSHRI KUMBAKUDI SUDHAKARAN: \nDR. A. CHALLAKUMAR: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is of the view that interfaith marriages are \nhappening due to forceful conversions; \n \n(b) if so, whether sufficient evidence have been collected by the \nGovernment which shows that interfaith marriages in India are connected to \ninstances of forced religious conversion; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) whether the Government intends to propose a central Anti Conversion \nLaw to curb interfaith marriages; \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof including the proposed date for introducing such \na law? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY)  \n(a) to (c) ‘Public Order’ and ‘Police’ are  State subjects as per the Seventh \nSchedule to the Constitution of India. Hence prevention, detection, \nregistration,  investigation  and  prosecution  of offences related to religious  \n\n--2-- \nL.S.US.Q.NO. 42 FOR 02.02.2021 \nconversions are primarily the concerns of the State Governments/Union \nTerritory (UT) Administrations. Action is taken as per existing laws by the \nlaw enforcing agencies whenever instances of violation come to notice.  \n(d)  In view of reply of part (a) to (c), above No. Sir \n(e) In view of reply of part (d) question does not arise \n****** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S5_U_43": "O.I\n \n.H. \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n43\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 2\nND\n \nFEBRUARY, 2021\n \n \nINCREASE IN CROP INSURANCE PREMIUM\n \n \n43. \n \nDR. BHARATIBEN DHIRUBHAI SHIYAL: \n \nSHRI SHANKAR LALWANI: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE   \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state: \n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the insurance premium amount has been increased under Pradhan Mantri \nFasal Bim\na Yojana (PMFBY) by expanding its coverage; \n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details of the increased amount; and \n \n \n(c) \n \nthe details of the area of land covered under PMFBY in Gujarat and Madhya Pradesh?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n   \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n(a) & (b):\n \nActuarial/bidded premium rate is charged by tahe insurance companies under \nthe Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY), but farmers have to pay maximum of 2% of \nsum insured for Kharif and 1.5% \nfor Rabi food and oilseed crops and 5% for \ncommercial/horticultural crops. Remaining part of actuarial premium is shared Central and \nState government on 50:50 basis except in North\n-\nEastern States where it is 90:10 with effect \nfrom Kharif 20202 season. The \npremium amount payable to insurance com\npanies also \ndepends on sum insu\nred and coverage under insured crops.\n \n \n(c):\n \n29.44 lakh ha. and 110.25 lakh ha. of cropped area is covered in Gujarat and Madhya \nPradesh respectively during 2019\n-\n20 under PMFBY.\n \n \n*****\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S5_U_44": "\u0001\n\u0002\u0003\u0004\u0005\u0006\u0007\b\u0005\u0007\t\u0001\u0003\n\u0001\u000b\u0007\f\u000b\r\n\u0001\n\b\u000b\u0007\u000b\u000e\t\u0006\u000f\u0001\u0003\n\u0001\b\u000b\u0010\u0006\u0003\u0011\u0001\u000e\b\r\u0012\u0012\u0001\r\u0007\f\u0001\b\u0005\f\u000b\u0013\b\u0001\u0005\u0007\t\u0005\u0006\u0014\u0006\u000b\u000e\u0005\u000e\n\u0001\n\u0001\n LOKSABHA\nK\nSUANNTRKEDTSUQLIKIL.K44\nK\nULKBTKAISJTNTRKLICKF4.FP.PFPW\nK\nK\nNTMQMA KL\u0019K\u001a LSTRK\u001bS\u001bT\u001c\nK\nK\n\u0001\n\u0015\n\u0016\u0016\n\u0017\n\u0001\u0001\u0001\u0001\u0001\u0001\u0001\u0001\u0001\u0001\u0001\u0001\u0001\u0001\u0001\n\u0001\n\u000e\u0018\u0006\u000b\u0001\u0006\r\u0018\u0013\u0012\u0001\u0006\r\b\u0005\u000e\u0018\u0001\u000e\u0018\u0005\u0019\r\u0012\u0005\u001a\n\u0001\n\u0001\n\u0001\n\u000e\u0018\u0006\u000b\u0001\u0003\b\u0014\u0006\r\u001b\r\u000e\u0018\u0001\u001c\u0018\u0013\u0014\r\u0012\u000e\u000b\u0007\u0018\u0001\u001d\u001e\u001f\u001d \n\u0001\n\u0014\r\u0019\r\u0007\u0001\n\u0006\r!\u0005\u0007\u000b\b\u001c\r\u0012\u001b\r\u0006\n\u0001\n\u0001\n\u0019\u001f\u001e\u001e\u0001\"#$\u0001\b\u001f%\u001f \"$&\u0001'(\u0001\b\u000b\u0010\u0006\u0003\u0011\u0001\u000e\b\r\u0012\u0012\u0001\r\u0007\f\u0001\b\u0005\f\u000b\u0013\b\u0001\u0005\u0007\t\u0005\u0006\u0014\u0006\u000b\u000e\u0005\u000e\u0001)$\u0001*\u001e$\u001d $+\u0001\"'\u0001 \"\u001d\"$\u001a\n\u0001\nK\n,\n\u001d\n-\nक\n\"#$\u0001 +$\"\u001d\u001f\u001e \u0001 \u001d%+\u0001 %./)$&\u0001 '(\u0001 \b\u001f0&'\u0011\u0001 \u000e/\u001d\u001e\u001e\u0001 \u001d%+\u0001 \b$+\u001f./\u0001 \u0005%\"$&*&\u001f $ \u0001 ,\b\u000e\b\u0005 -\u0001 0\u001e' $+\u0001 \u001f%\u0001 \"#$\u0001\n0'.%\"&1\u0001 \u001f%0$\u0001\"#$\u0001*$&\u001f'+\u0001'(\u0001%\u001d\"\u001f'%2\u001f+$\u0001\u001e'03+'2%\u0001+.$\u0001\"'\u0001\u0010\u0003\u0004\u000b\f\n4\n56\u0001\u0014\u001d%+$/\u001f0\u0011\u0001\u000e\"\u001d\"\n$7\u0013\t\n4\n2\u001f $8\n\u0001\n,\n)\n-\nक\n\"#$\u00010