    "ls|LS17_S5_U_9": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, \nCOOPERATION\n \n& FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n \n9\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n  \n2\nND\n \nFEBRUARY, 2021\n \n \nPOLICY FOR DRIP IRRIGATION\n \n \n*9. \n   \nSHRI SANJAY KAKA PATIL: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS \nWELFARE\n  \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \nbe pleased to state\n:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government has introduced a new policy for drip irrigation as per a \ngeographical survey all over the \ncountry;\n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details thereof; \n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Union Government has sanctioned fund for drip irrigation schemes \nintroduced in Maharashtra, if so, the details thereof; \n \n \n(d) \n \nwhether the Government proposes to sanction and release fund for alkaline soil in \nMaharashtra, especially Sangli district; and \n \n \n(e) \n \nif so, the details thereof?\n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n \n \n  \n(SHRI \nNARENDRA  SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n \n(a) to (\ne\n):\n \nA statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (\ne\n) OF L\nOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n  \n9\n   \nDUE FOR REPLY ON \n2\nND\n \nFEBRUARY, 2021\n.\n \n \n(a) & (b)\n:\n \n \nThe Department of Agriculture, Cooperation & Farmers Welfare (DAC&FW) \nis implementing a Centrally Sponsored Scheme of  Per Drop More Crop component of \nPradhan \n \nMantri\n \n \nKrishi \n \nSinchayee \n \nYojana (PMKSY\n-\nPDMC) from 2015\n-\n16 in all the States \nof the country wh\nich focuses on enhancing water use efficiency at farm level through Micro \nIrrigation viz. Drip and Sprinkler irrigation systems.\n \nBesides promoting Micro Irrigation, this \ncomponent also supports micro level water storage or water conservation/management \nact\nivities to supplement source creation for Micro Irrigation.\n \n \n \nAn area of 51.54 lakh ha has been covered under Micro Irrigation (Drip irrigation \n27.26 lakh ha & Sprinkler Irrigation 24.28 lakh ha) in the country through PMKSY\n-\nPDMC \nfrom 2015\n-\n16 to till date.\n \nFurther, 4.84 lakh micro level water harvesting / secondary \nstorage structures have been created under the scheme to supplement the micro irrigation.\n \n \n(c)\n:\n \nUnder PMKSY\n-\n \nPDMC, Central Assistance of Rs. 1660.4 crores  has been released \nto the State of Mahar\nashtra from 2015\n-\n16 till date including\n \nRs. 200.00 crore  released \nduring the current financial year.  \n \n \n(d) & (e)\n:    \nThe Department of Agriculture, Cooperation & Farmer’s Welfare is \nimplementing Reclamation of Problem Soils (Alkali/Saline &\n \nAcid) as a sub Scheme of \nRashtriya\n \n \nKrishi\n \n \nVikas\n \nYojana (RKVY) on Pilot basis since 2016\n-\n17 in 15 States including \nMaharashtra.\n \n \n \nUnder the scheme, Central Assistance of Rs. 1.32 crore was released to State\n \nof \nMaharashtra during 2018\n-\n19 for implementatio\nn of a project of sub\n-\nsurface drainage for \nreclamation of saline soils in \n \nKolhapur District\n \nas per annual action plan approved by State \n \nContd…2/\n-\n \n \n\n-\n2\n-\n \nLevel Sanctioning Committee (SLSC)\n \nof RKVY. For the current year, Central Assistance of \nRs. 1.5 crore h\nas been allocated to State to implement the scheme.\n  \nCentral Assistance \ncould not be released during 2019\n-\n20 and 2020\n-\n21 as State has not submitted any project \nproposal\n \nthrough Annual Action Plans for these years and progress & utilisation certificates \nfor\n \nearlier released amount.\n \n \n*******\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S5_U_90": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*90 \nANSWERED ON 08.02.2021 \n \nREVIVAL OF TOURISM \n \n*90. SHRI KALYAN BANERJEE:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether it is a fact that COVID -19 has put 85 milli on tourism \njobs in the country at risk;  \n(b)  whether the United Nations World Tourism Organization has \nestimated a reduction of 58 per cent to 78 per cent in tourist \ntraffic across the world and in India;  \n(c)  whether the Tourism Association in the count ry and the State \nGovernment Departments have proposed to redefine, refocus \nand change for going forward towards survive, revive and \nthrive to tourism for their survival therein; and  \n(d)  if so, the details of proposals received and action taken by the \nGovernment thereon? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n  \n*********** \n  \n  \n  \n  \n \n\nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO  PARTS (a) to (d) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTI ON NO. *90  ANSWERED ON 08.02.2021 \nREGARDING REVIVAL OF TOURISM \n  \n(a): Ministry of Tourism, Government of India has engaged National \nCouncil of Applied Economic Research (NCAER) in January, 2021  to \nconduct study on “ India and the Coronavirus Pandemic: Econo mic \nLosses for Households Engaged in Tourism and Policies for \nRecovery”. The objectives of the study includes following: \n  \ni. Quantifying the sector -wise and overall loss in income of the \neconomy and of the household sector and also the loss in jobs, \ndue t o the impact of Coronavirus pandemic on the tourism \nsector  \n  \nii. Propose policy measures appropriate to provide relief to the \ntourism sector, in general, and households involved in tourism \nrelated activities, in particular, based on feedback received \nfrom various stakeholders. \n \n(b): Yes, Sir.  As per UNWTO World Tourism barometer for May 2020, \nUNWTO estimated a reduction of 58 per cent to 78 per cent in \ntourist traffic across the world. Three scenarios were  assumed on \nthe basis of possible dates of lifting  of travel    restrictions and \ngradual re-opening of international borders. \n \ni. Scenario 1 (-58% in arrivals in 2020) if travel restrictions lifted \nin early July   \nii. Scenario 2 ( -70% in 2020) based on the same in early \nSeptember \niii. Scenario 3 ( -78% in 2020) based o n the same in early \nDecember \nAdditionally, according to UNWTO World Tourism barometer \npublished in January 2021, UNWTO estimated a reduction of 73.9% \nin 2020 over the previous year International tourist arrivals (overnight \n\nvisitors) across the world. Where as, UNWTO estimated a reduction \nof 53.5% International tourist arrivals (overnight visitors) to India in \n2020 over the previous year. \n  \n(c) & (d):  With a view to prepare for a post -COVID 19 revival, the \nMinistry of Tourism  issued Operational Recommendation s for \ndifferent segments of Tourism Service Providers in the Travel sector \n which are formulated in consultation with State Governments, \ntourism / hospitality stakeholders and guidelines issued time to time \nby the Ministry of Health for facilitating smooth and safe resumption \nof business. \n            \nFurther, the Ministry of Tourism has issued modified guidelines \nfor Recognition of Tourism Service Providers dated 08.12.2020 \nwhich would be effective from January, 2021. As per the modified \nguidelines, Categor y of Greenshoot / Start -up- Agencies is being \nintroduced for the first time. This is in keeping with the policy of the \nGovt. of India for encouraging Start -Ups and will also further the \ncause of ‘Atmanirbhar India’. \n  \nWith an aim to incentivize stakeholder s in the Tourism \nindustry, the Guidelines for the Scheme of Market Development \nAssistance (MDA) for providing financial support to stakeholders for \nthe promotion of Domestic Tourism have been modified to enhance \nthe scope and reach of the scheme, so as to provide maximum \nbenefits to the stakeholders. Additional promotional activities have \nbeen incorporated including online promotions and the extent of \nfinancial assistance permissible has been enhanced.  \n****** \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S5_U_91": " \n����������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������\n��\n������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\nPayment of States’ GST Share \n \n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF FINANCE \n \n(SMT. NIRMALA SITHARAMAN) \n \n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (c) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *91 DUE FOR ANSWER ON 08th FEBRUARY, 2021 \n \n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������\n��\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n****** ",
    "ls|LS17_S5_U_92": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *92 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 08TH FEBRUARY, 2021 \n \nJOB FAIRS ORGANISED IN MADHYA PRADESH \n \n*92.  DR. VIRENDRA KUMAR: \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Union Government has prepared any plan with the State \nGovernments to organize job fairs at district level across the country ; and  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof including the number of job fairs organized in \nMadhya Pradesh during the last three years, district -wise and the outcome \nthereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR) \n \n(a) to (b): A statement is laid on the Table of the House.   \n** \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (b) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *92 DUE FOR REPLY ON 08 -02-2021 BY DR. \nVIRENDRA KUMAR REGARDING “JOB FAIRS IN MADHYA PRADESH”. \n(a) to (b): Government is implementing the National Career Service (NCS) \nProject to provide a variety of employment related services like job \nmatching, career counselling, vocational guidance, information on skill \ndevelopment courses, etc. The NCS project comprises three components:  \n1)  National Career Service (NCS) Portal (www.ncs.gov.in). \n \n2) Model Career Centres (MCC) having state of art infrastructure to \nprovide all employment related services including outreach \nactivities and job fairs in collaboration with States/UTs and other \ninstitutions. The Government provides financial assistance to these \ncentres for an amount up to Rs. 50 Lakh per centre based on the \nproposal of State/UT/Institutions  as per scheme guidelines. As of \nnow the G overnment has approved 207 Model Career Centres \n(MCCS) (including 7 on Non -funded basis).Out of the total 207 MCCs, \n10 MCCs have been approved in Madhya Pradesh. \n \n3) Interlinking of Employment Exchanges- Under this component, funds \nhave been provided to State s/UTs to interlink Employment \nExchanges with NCS Portal and to organize job fairs. Till date, funds \nhave been released to 28 States/UTs as per scheme guidelines.  \n \nThe details of the job fairs organized through NCS in collaboration with \nStates/UTs includin g Madhya Pradesh is attached at Annexure . Besides \nthis, State Governments also organize job fairs from their resources as per \ntheir requirements.  \n  \n\n \nAnnexure \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *92 \nDUE FOR REPLY ON 08.02.2021 BY DR. VIRENDRA KUMAR  REGARDING \"JOB FAIRS \nORGANISED IN MADHYA PRADESH\" \nNo. of Job Fairs Organized in States/UTs during last three years \nSl. \nNo. \nStates/UTs No. of \nJob \nFairs \nNo. of \nEmployers \nwho \nparticipated \nNo. of \nvacancies \nNo. of \njobseek\ners \nwho \nparticip\nated \nNo. of \ncandidat\nes \nshortliste\nd for next \nround \nNo. of \ncandidat\nes given \nfinal offer \nletter \n1 Andaman and Nicobar 4 1844 94 3242 944 369 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 127 6252 5728 11924 4163 2466 \n3 Assam 114 12331 7566 70286 12099 2256 \n4 Bihar 125 24070 33463 107655 22257 3608 \n5 Chhattisgarh 214 21281 29880 31058 9570 4293 \n6 Delhi 17 16690 2905 27039 9724 327 \n7 Goa 2 3272 2104 10334 2974 289 \n8 Gujarat 450 159246 88122 326720 169622 126246 \n9 Haryana 104 44267 15864 49022 15826 5584 \n10 Himachal Pradesh 88 15752 5046 17795 5157 4899 \n11 Jammu and Kashmir 18 230 635 2325 493 123 \n12 Jharkhand 90 44080 24344 123906 63544 23695 \n13 Karnataka 44 36905 8914 35020 11261 5607 \n14 Kerala 36 4818 11044 21367 6288 74 \n15 Madhya Pradesh 416 135009 74596 222209 106936 28848 \n16 Maharashtra 116 67947 28355 78136 34390 8127 \n17 Manipur 52 3320 428 1769 1232 138 \n18 Nagaland 1 12 1 12 12 12 \n19 Odisha 217 58199 21622 65118 24881 6453 \n20 Punjab 265 15233 10151 44162 13252 14033 \n21 Rajasthan 119 37092 26129 92248 31647 11773 \n22 Sikkim 5 88 14 195 88 40 \n23 Tamil Nadu 327 149214 68829 129758 53257 37782 \n24 Telangana 158 39068 59259 78523 26311 1651 \n25 Tripura 18 9312 58 2882 591 114 \n26 Uttar Pradesh 347 114341 62672 169268 51286 28865 \n27 Uttarakhand 78 27314 20112 47645 19203 8041 \n28 West Bengal 37 3656 324 7439 2519 303 \nTotal 3589 1050843 608259 1777057 699527 326016 \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S5_U_93": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 93 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 2ND FEBRUARY, 2021/ MAGHA 13, 1942 (SAKA) \n \nPROTESTING FARMERS \n \n93.  SHRI K. MURALEEDHARAN: \nSHRI KUMBAKUDI SUDHAKARAN: \nSHRI BALUBHAU ALIAS SURESH \nNARAYAN DHANORKAR: \nSHRI MOHAMMED FAIZAL P.P.: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether any case has been registered against the farmers protesting the \nrecently passed ‘Farm Laws’ between September to December, 2020 at Delhi \nborder; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) the reasons for use of tear gas and resorting to Lathi charge against \nprotesting farmers by the Delhi Police; \n \n(d) whether the Government is aware about the number of farmers who died \nor committed suicide in protest at the Delhi border; and \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY)  \n \n(a) to (e) : 'Public Order' and 'Police' are State subjects as per the Seventh \nSchedule to the Constitution of India. The responsibilities of maintaining law \nand order, including investigation, registration/ prosecution of crimes, \nconviction of accused, protection of life and property etc. rest primarily with  \n \n\n-2- \nL.S.US.Q NO. 93 FOR 02.02.2021   \nthe respective State Governments. Central Government keeps a constant \nwatch on the internal security scenario of the country through its security \nagencies and responds appropriately to the emergent situation. Among other \nmeasures, alerts and advisories are issued to the law enforcement agencies \nwhenever any threat to internal security is perceived. The Central \nGovernment also provides Central Armed Police Forces to the States/Union \nTerritories upon request. \n \nIn so far NCT of Delhi is concerned, Delhi Police has informed that 39 cases \nhave been registered against the farmers protesting the recently passed \n‘Farm Laws’ between September to December, 2020 at Delhi border. Delhi \nPolice has also informed that one suicide case has been reported to them \nduring the protest against Farm Laws at the Delhi border. \n \nAs regards use of tear gas and resorting to Lathi charge against protesting \nfarmers by the Delhi Police, it has been reported by Delhi Police that at Delhi \nborder, large convoys of protesting farmers in tractor trolleys tried to \nfuriously force their way and go past Police barricades to enter Delhi to mark \ntheir protest against the recently enacted ‘Farm Laws’. They aggressively \nresorted to rioting, damage to Government property and used criminal force \nto  deter public  servants  from the discharge of their duty, thereby  inflicting  \n \n \n\n\\-3- \nL.S.US.Q NO. 93 FOR 02.02.2021 \ninjuries to the on-duty police personnel. Moreover, social distancing was not \nfollowed by the farmers/protestors and they gathered in large numbers \nwithout face masks amid Covid-19 pandemic. The farmers’ actions left the \nDelhi Police with no option but to use tear gas, water cannons and mild force \nto control the crowd. \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S5_U_94": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n9\n4\n                     \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n2\nND \nFEBRUARY\n,\n \n20\n21\n \n \nAVERAGE MARKET PRICES FOR CROPS \n \n \n94. \n \nDR. A. CHALLAKUMAR:\n \n \nSHRI UTTAM KUMAR REDDY NALAMADA: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a)\n \nthe average market prices realised by farmers for\n \nIndia‘s mai\nn agricultural and \nhorticulture \ncrops since\n \n2014, State\n-\nwise and year\n-\nwise;\n \n \n(b)\n \nthe difference between the prices realised and\n \nthe \nMSP announced by the Government \nState\n-\nwise and\n \nyear\n-\nwise since 2014;\n \n \n(c)\n \nthe reasons for the difference; and\n \n \n(d)\n \nthe impac\nt of the Pradhan Mantri Annadata Aay\n \nSanrakshan Abhiyan scheme in \naddressing this problem?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \nमं\u0000ी\n  \n   \n       \n( SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n  \n \n \n(a) \nto\n \n(\nc\n)\n:\n \nYear\n-\nwise weighted average prices (wholesale) of main agricultural and \nhorticulture crops in the mandis of major  producing states as per market information entered \nby respective Agricultural Produce Market Committee (APMC) markets under respective \nStates/\n \nUnion Territories (UTs) along with the price difference as compare to MSP (wherein \napplicable) since 2014 \nis \nattached \nat \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n.\n \nMSP is \nannounced\n \nby the Government for \n2\n2\n \nagricultural commodities. \nThere is no Minimum Support Price for horticulture cro\nps. The \nwholesale market price of crop realized depends on the demand, supply and quality of the \ncrop at any point of time. The difference of wholesale market price if any with respect to MSP \narises due to above. \n \n(d):\n \nPradhan Mantri Annadata Aay SanraksHan Abhiyan’ \n(\nPM\n-\nAASHA) is an umbrella \nscheme to ensure Minimum Support Price (MSP) to farmers. It comprises the erstwhile Price \nSupport Scheme (PSS) with certain\n \nmodifications and rolling out of new schemes of Price\n \nCont\nd….2/\n-\n \n\n-\n2\n-\n \n \nDeficiency Payment Scheme (PDPS) and pilot of Private Procurement and Stockist Scheme \n(PPSS). \n \nUnder PM\n-\nAASHA, States/UTs are offered to choose either PSS and PDPS in a \ngiven procurement season with respect to particular oilseeds crop for the e\nntire State. Pulses \nand Copra are procured under PSS. Only one scheme i.e. PSS or PDPS may be made \noperational in one State with respect to one commodity. Further, states have the option to roll \nout PPSS on pilot basis in district/selected APMCs of distric\nt involving the participation of \nprivate stockist for oilseeds. Further, wheat paddy and coarse grains are procured under the \nexisting schemes of Department of Food and Public Distribution and Cotton is procured under \nthe existing schemes of Ministry of Te\nxtiles. \n \nPM\n-\nAASHA helps the farmers in getting MSP for \ntheir crop which are procured\n.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \n \nYear wise weighted average prices of Main Agricultural &\n \nHorticulture Crops in mandis \nof major producing State from 2014\n-\n15 to 2019\n-\n20. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n1. Commodity : Sesamum\n \n \n \n \n \n \n(In Rs/ Qtl)\n \n \n \n \n \nData\n \n \n \n \n \nState\n \nyear\n \nWAP* \n \nMSP **\n \nDiff.(WAP\n-\nMSP)\n \nGujarat\n \n2014\n-\n15\n \n10139\n \n4600\n \n5539\n \n \n \n2015\n-\n16\n \n8333\n \n4700\n \n3633\n \n \n \n2016\n-\n17\n \n6986\n \n5000\n \n1986\n \n \n \n2017\n-\n18\n \n8062\n \n5300\n \n2762\n \n \n \n2018\n-\n19\n \n12925\n \n6249\n \n6676\n \n \n \n